# TASK-1973 — a fixture is a byte contract, so its bytes must not depend on who
# checked it out.
#
# `packages/web/src/lib/__fixtures__/fence-agreement.md` is checked in to BOTH this
# repo and choda-deck, and TASK-1943's cross-repo test asserts the two
# implementations of `listMermaidFences` agree on every fence body derived from it.
# With `core.autocrlf=true` — the Windows default — git rewrote those 73 line
# endings to CRLF on checkout. The parser keeps each slice VERBATIM (deliberately,
# so a caller rewriting a fence cannot silently convert the file to LF), so the
# bodies came back carrying a trailing \r and the pinned table, written with \n, no
# longer matched.
#
# The parser is right and the test is right; the file was being mutated underneath
# both. Pinning eol=lf makes the fixture literally the same bytes everywhere, which
# is the only reading under which "byte-for-byte identical in both repositories"
# means anything. The twin of this file lives in choda-deck/.gitattributes and the
# two must stay in step — that is the whole premise of the fixture.
#
# Scoped to fixtures on purpose: a blanket `*.md eol=lf` would renormalise every
# document in docs/knowledge on the next checkout.
**/__fixtures__/*.md eol=lf
